<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Where the werewolf comes from: EVERY biome, one entry each, and which COLOUR appears is decided
     here by pointing each biome at a different group. It is not a creature of one region - it is a
     thing that happens at night, wherever you are - but it wears the region.

       snow                                  animalWerewolfIce     the snow pelt, brightened
       desert                                animalWerewolfBrown
       pine forest, burnt forest, wasteland  animalWerewolf        the dark one

     One entry per biome is how this file works; there is no "all biomes" element, and the five
     blocks below are the whole of it. All three colours are the same creature - same stats, same
     AI, same howl - and the DLL treats them as one for its limits.

     ON THE NUMBERS
     maxcount 1 - at most one alive per 80x80 m spawn area at a time. It calls its own reinforcements
     (see WerewolfHowl.cs), so the spawner does not need to provide more than one.

     respawndelay is vanilla's own wasteland enemy-animal figure, untouched. It is NOT the knob for
     making the creature rarer, and using it as one was a mistake worth recording.

     The unit is game days - vanilla's own header says "game days - .000278 real seconds, .0167
     mins", which is just 1 real second and 1 real minute expressed in days at the default
     60-minute day. But the delay is counted PER SPAWN AREA, one 5x5-chunk 80x80 m block, each with
     its own timer in ChunkAreaBiomeSpawnData. It only bites on a player who stays put. A player
     running crosses fresh areas whose timers were never started, so they spawn at once - which is
     why five turned up in five minutes of running with the delay at three days, and why raising it
     to eight changed nothing about that.

     Rarity therefore lives entirely in the entity group's probability (2 in 100, entitygroups.xml)
     and in the hard world-wide cap of one at a time (WerewolfSpawnGate.AmbientLimit). Both of
     those are indifferent to whether the player is moving.

     TIME AND PROGRESSION ARE NOT GATED HERE, and cannot be:

       * time is "Any" because this file only understands Day / Night / Any, and its Night is dusk
         to dawn (22:00-04:00 at the default 18-hour daylight), not the 21:00-05:00 wanted.
       * game stage has NO representation in biome spawning at all. SpawnManagerBiomes.SpawnUpdate
         consults exactly two things, World.IsDaytime() and the game difficulty setting - there is
         no game stage check anywhere in that path. Vanilla gates its dangerous ambient spawns by
         PLACE instead: the demolisher rides in ZombiesDowntown at 2% and needs a downtown POI, and
         the zombie bear needs the wasteland. Game stage only ever appears on the horde side, in
         gamestages.xml.

     Both are therefore enforced in the DLL, in WerewolfSpawnGate.cs, which turns away any werewolf
     ARRIVING outside the 22:00-03:00 window or below world game stage 125 - about day 50, just
     under the 147 at which vanilla first lets a demolisher into a blood moon. One already in the
     world is left alone and keeps hunting past 03:00. The cost of "Any" here is that a daytime or
     too-early spawn is created and removed again a second later, out of sight; only the biome the
     players are standing in ever spawns, so that is a handful of wasted entities per in-game day
     and nothing visible. -->
<configs>

    <append xpath="/spawning/biome[@name='pine_forest']">
        <spawn id="ww01" maxcount="1" respawndelay="0.9,1.575,1.215,0.9,0.585,0.315" time="Any" entitygroup="WerewolfNight" type="animal"/>
    </append>

    <append xpath="/spawning/biome[@name='burnt_forest']">
        <spawn id="ww01" maxcount="1" respawndelay="0.9,1.575,1.215,0.9,0.585,0.315" time="Any" entitygroup="WerewolfNight" type="animal"/>
    </append>

    <append xpath="/spawning/biome[@name='desert']">
        <spawn id="ww01" maxcount="1" respawndelay="0.9,1.575,1.215,0.9,0.585,0.315" time="Any" entitygroup="WerewolfNightDesert" type="animal"/>
    </append>

    <append xpath="/spawning/biome[@name='snow']">
        <spawn id="ww01" maxcount="1" respawndelay="0.9,1.575,1.215,0.9,0.585,0.315" time="Any" entitygroup="WerewolfNightSnow" type="animal"/>
    </append>

    <append xpath="/spawning/biome[@name='wasteland']">
        <spawn id="ww01" maxcount="1" respawndelay="0.9,1.575,1.215,0.9,0.585,0.315" time="Any" entitygroup="WerewolfNight" type="animal"/>
    </append>

</configs>
